  Next fork, and it's the one that decides whether this actually removes duplication.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how much of a form the shared layer owns. Right now [38;5;153mapp.js[39m hand-writes the submit listener, reads each input
  by id, calls [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m, and reports failures with [38;5;153mconsole.error[39m. If the shared layer only owns the rules, every
  new form re-writes that wiring and invents its own way to show errors — the duplication moves instead of going away.

  - [1mRules only[0m — exports rule functions and a [38;5;153mvalidate(values, schema)[39m returning [38;5;153m{valid, errors: {field: message}}[39m.
    Smallest surface, trivially testable, zero assumptions about your markup. Cost: each form still hand-writes its
    listener, its id lookups, and its error rendering. With several forms planned, that's the part you'd be
    copy-pasting.
  - [1mRules + form binding[0m — one call like [38;5;153mbindForm(formEl, schema, onValid)[39m that wires submit, pulls values, validates,
    and writes messages into the DOM. Removes the actual repetition. Cost: it imposes a markup convention (an error
    element per field), so [38;5;153mindex.html[39m changes and every future form must follow the convention.
  - [1mBoth, layered[0m — a pure [38;5;153mvalidate(values, schema)[39m core plus a separate, optional [38;5;153mbindForm[39m built on it. Forms that fit
    the convention get one-line wiring; anything unusual drops to the core. Cost: two exports instead of one, slightly
    more indirection.

  [1mMy recommendation: both, layered.[0m The pure core is what I can unit-test properly and what keeps the rules independent
  of your markup, but the binding helper is what makes form number four cheap to write. Splitting them means the DOM
  convention is opt-in rather than a condition of using the validation at all — so an odd form later doesn't force us to
  widen the framework.
